                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.36729/2610.36729v1/Hy-Nomas-Bench_overview_clear.png",
                "caption": "Figure 10:Overview of Hy-Nomas-Bench.Top:Model outputs for an example case from Bench-3 (interleaved text–camera control).Bottom:(a) World settings span diverse scenes, visual styles,\nsubjects, and viewpoints. (b) 280 cases cover multi-turn text interaction, camera navigation,\ninterleaved control, and spatial memory.\n(c) Unified control maps instructions to text, binary key states[T,8][T,8](W/A/S/Dand four arrow keys), or per-frame4×44\\times 4camera-to-world matrices[T,4,4][T,4,4], whereTTis the\nframe count. Generation continues across turns without world resets.\n(d) Three annotators evaluate prompt following, physical plausibility,\ncamera control, and memory consistency through pairwise comparisons.\nResults are summarized by win/tie/loss rates and net win rate\n(win minus loss, in percentage points).",
                "position": 1351
            },
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2610.36729/2610.36729v1/Hy-Nomas-Bench4_indoor.png",
                "caption": "Figure 11:Illustration of the design of Bench-4: Spatial memory.Each uses a static\nanchor object and four outbound–return pairs. Bench-4\nevaluates object permanence under different camera movements, and appearance/layout consistency.Left:Different trajectory types.Center:Scene, camera frustum, and trajectory.Right:Inference results by  Hy-Nomas.",
